WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.240
Could the same pencil seem longer just because someone moved the ruler?

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:07.600
Imagine two friends measuring it from different marks.

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:12.577
Before we trust a number, let us investigate the unit, the scale and the starting point.

00:00:12.577 --> 00:00:17.172
Our learning goal is to measure length accurately using useful metric units.

00:00:17.172 --> 00:00:18.732
Success criteria.

00:00:18.732 --> 00:00:21.772
I can choose a sensible unit and instrument.

00:00:21.772 --> 00:00:24.892
I can read a ruler from the correct starting point.

00:00:24.892 --> 00:00:28.429
I can combine metres and centimetres accurately.

00:00:28.429 --> 00:00:29.109
Remember that a measure counts equal units.

00:00:29.109 --> 00:00:38.549
Millimetres help with small details, centimetres suit a pencil, metres suit a room, and kilometres suit a long journey.

00:00:38.549 --> 00:00:41.029
Choose a tool with useful markings.

00:00:41.029 --> 00:00:45.592
Smaller units can give a more precise measurement, rather than changing the object.

00:00:45.592 --> 00:00:50.232
A ribbon begins at the two centimetre mark and ends at nine centimetres.

00:00:50.232 --> 00:00:51.592
Predict its length.

00:00:51.592 --> 00:00:57.981
Count the equal one-centimetre intervals between those marks, rather than reading the final label alone.

00:00:57.981 --> 00:01:00.861
There are seven centimetres between the marks.

00:01:00.861 --> 00:01:03.181
Nine minus two equals seven.

00:01:03.181 --> 00:01:10.125
Moving the ruler does not stretch the ribbon: subtract the starting reading whenever the object is not lined up with zero.

00:01:10.125 --> 00:01:14.405
A display board measures two metres and thirty-five centimetres.

00:01:14.405 --> 00:01:17.005
We want a single centimetre reading.

00:01:17.005 --> 00:01:23.112
One metre contains one hundred centimetres, so the two metres account for two hundred centimetres.

00:01:23.112 --> 00:01:25.992
Add the remaining thirty-five centimetres.

00:01:25.992 --> 00:01:30.552
Two hundred plus thirty-five makes two hundred and thirty-five centimetres.

00:01:30.552 --> 00:01:35.147
The combined measure and the single-unit measure describe exactly the same length.

00:01:35.147 --> 00:01:39.587
Reading nine centimetres as the ribbon length ignores where it started.

00:01:39.587 --> 00:01:41.667
Count the space between the marks.

00:01:41.667 --> 00:01:46.161
A different starting mark changes the subtraction, but not the physical ribbon.

00:01:46.161 --> 00:01:50.641
A strip starts at three centimetres and ends at eleven centimetres.

00:01:50.641 --> 00:01:51.961
How long is it?

00:01:51.961 --> 00:01:55.100
Look at the model, and keep your prediction in mind.

00:01:55.100 --> 00:01:55.660
Eleven minus three is eight centimetres.

00:01:55.660 --> 00:02:03.865
Check by counting eight equal intervals, or placing a zero-start ruler beside the same strip.

00:02:03.865 --> 00:02:08.225
Choose a useful unit, read the interval size and check the starting mark.

00:02:08.225 --> 00:02:13.345
For a practical investigation, measure a pencil in centimetres and millimetres.

00:02:13.345 --> 00:02:16.985
Discuss which reading describes its length more precisely.

00:02:16.985 --> 00:02:19.490
Reflect on which model helped you and why.
